# Long-running processes: check progress, not liveness

Unscoped on purpose: long jobs get launched from any session, whatever the
files in scope are.

On 2026-09-01 an `igir` hash pass was left running against a ROM library. It
was checked several times over 19 hours and reported as healthy every time,
because the checks asked the wrong question: the process was alive and burning
80% CPU. It had in fact been looping on one 495 MB file since minute five - a
file a plain `dd` reads end to end in 29 seconds. Nineteen hours of CPU, no
output, and every status report said it was fine.

A process being alive says nothing. `%CPU` says nothing: a tight loop pegs the
CPU exactly like real work does. Elapsed time says nothing on its own. Before
reporting a long job as healthy, name the artifact that moved since the last
check:

- the output file or cache grew, or its mtime advanced
- the log gained lines
- the file currently open (`lsof -p <pid>`) is not the one open last time
- a counter the job itself prints went up

If nothing moved, the job is stalled, however busy it looks. Say so instead of
reporting progress, and diagnose before relaunching: read the input the job
was chewing on with an independent tool, so a bug in the job is not mistaken
for a corrupt file or a failing disk.

Arm the check when launching, not after something looks wrong. A job expected
to run for hours needs a stall detector from the start - a watcher comparing
the open file or the output mtime across intervals - or it can burn a whole
night unobserved. Cap the cost of a single pathological input where the job
allows it.

## Counting is a progress signal too, and shell globs lie

A shell glob silently produces nothing once the match list is long enough to
overflow the argument list. On the same day, `ls dir/*.spc | wc -l` printed 0
while 16,401 matching files were still sitting there, which read exactly like
"the move finished". Count with `find <dir> -name '<pattern>' | wc -l`
instead, and treat a sudden clean zero from a glob as suspect until `find`
confirms it.
